      "removed_subtitle": "Original course contributor: Iwan Kartawira, NSI Manager at IPronics.",
      "removed_module_credit": "Iwan Kartawira, NSI Manager at IPronics",
      "removed_spoken_acknowledgement": "We recognize Iwan Kartawira at IPronics for technical guidance during the original course scope and early planning.",
      "new_script": "By the end of this module, you'll be able to describe an optical circuit without confusing it with a packet switch. You'll identify where optical-to-electrical conversion is avoided, and where electrical processing still occurs. You'll also explain why a bufferless component can reduce one source of delay without making a whole network contention-free. The goal is a practical vocabulary for the technology and deployment choices that follow.",
      "audio": {
        "script_file": "audio/module1/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.txt",
        "wav_file": "audio/module1/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.wav",
        "delivery_file": "audio/module1/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.mp3",
        "approved": true
      }
    },
    {
      "module": 2,
      "slide": 2,
      "removed_subtitle": "Original course contributor: Swapna Nannapaneni, Senior Engineering Manager at Lumentum.",
      "removed_module_credit": "Swapna Nannapaneni, Senior Engineering Manager at Lumentum",
      "removed_spoken_acknowledgement": "We recognize Swapna Nannapaneni at Lumentum for guidance during the design and development of the original technology course.",
      "new_script": "By the end of this module, you'll recognize the operating principle behind robotic, MEMS, liquid-crystal, piezoelectric, silicon-photonic and metasurface approaches. You'll also understand why hybrid designs combine mechanisms. More importantly, you'll be able to separate a family-level description from a particular device specification. A technology name does not guarantee a switching time or port count.",
      "audio": {
        "script_file": "audio/module2/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.txt",
        "wav_file": "audio/module2/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.wav",
        "delivery_file": "audio/module2/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.mp3",
        "approved": true
      }
    },
    {
      "module": 3,
      "slide": 2,
      "removed_subtitle": "Original course contributor: Ajay Gautham Manicka, Optical Transport Engineer at Nebius.",
      "removed_module_credit": "Ajay Gautham Manicka, Optical Transport Engineer at Nebius",
      "removed_spoken_acknowledgement": "We recognize Ajay Gautham Manicka at Nebius for technical guidance during the development of the original deployment course.",
      "new_script": "By the end of this module, you'll be able to locate the optical layer in two very different architectures. One connects groups of packet switches across a data center. Another connects compute Cubes into a configurable accelerator topology. You'll also explain how optical reconnection can help resilience, and why successful application recovery requires more than a new path.",
      "audio": {
        "script_file": "audio/module3/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.txt",
        "wav_file": "audio/module3/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.wav",
        "delivery_file": "audio/module3/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.mp3",
        "approved": true
      }
    },
    {
      "module": 4,
      "slide": 2,
      "removed_subtitle": "Original course contributor: Ajay Gautham Manicka, Optical Transport Engineer at Nebius.",
      "removed_module_credit": "Ajay Gautham Manicka, Optical Transport Engineer at Nebius",
      "removed_spoken_acknowledgement": "We again recognize Ajay Gautham Manicka at Nebius for technical guidance on the original emerging-use-cases course.",
      "new_script": "By the end of this module, you'll distinguish the available physical paths from the controller's allocation of capacity over those paths. You'll explain why a hybrid design can assign different traffic classes to different interconnect fabrics. You'll also separate the useful transparency of an optical path from stronger claims about quantum-state preservation or memory semantics.",
      "audio": {
        "script_file": "audio/module4/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.txt",
        "wav_file": "audio/module4/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.wav",
        "delivery_file": "audio/module4/slide_02_what_you_will_be_able_to_explain.mp3",
